<h2 id="the-abbot-they-tried-to-poison">The Abbot They Tried to Poison</h2>

<p>The monks had come to him first. Their abbot had died, and they had heard of a young hermit living in a cave near Subiaco, and they wanted him to rule them in the dead man’s place. He said no. Their ways, he told them, were different from his, and the two would never agree. They went on asking, and in the end he gave in.</p>

<p>It went as he had said it would. He held them to the regular life of monks, the life they had promised, and they found that they had called in a master who meant it. Before long some of them mixed poison into his wine. When the glass came to him, Benedict made the sign of the cross over it, and the glass broke in pieces, Gregory the Great writes, “as though the sign of the cross had been a stone thrown against it.”</p>

<p>He understood. He did not curse them or call down judgment. With what Gregory calls “a mild countenance and quiet mind,” he spoke to the men who had just tried to kill him:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Almighty God have mercy upon you, and forgive you: why have you used me in this manner? Did not I tell you before hand, that our manner of living could never agree together?”</p>
  <p><cite>— Benedict to the monks who poisoned his wine, in Gregory the Great, <em>Dialogues</em>, Book II, chapter 3 (English of 1608)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>“Go your ways,” he told them, “and seek ye out some other Father.” Then he left them and went back to the wilderness, where, in Gregory’s phrase, he dwelt with himself.</p>

<p>It is a strange beginning for a man whom a pope would one day call the father of Europe. The first community that asked Benedict to be its father would rather kill him than live under him. Yet fourteen centuries later, when Pope Paul VI named him principal patron of all Europe, he called him a messenger of peace, a builder of unity, a teacher of civilization, and the founder of monastic life in the West. The Rule that Benedict wrote for his later monks, which Gregory praised for its discretion, ends by asking them to bear one another’s weaknesses, of body and of character, with the greatest patience.</p>

<p>Between that broken glass and that patient chapter lies the whole of his life, and its question. Benedict went into the wilderness to be alone with God. But people would not leave him alone. A priest was sent to find him on Easter Day, shepherds took him for a wild animal, Roman nobles brought him their sons, a Gothic king came to test him, and his own sister kept him talking through a night of thunder. How did a man who ran from the world into a cave become the father of monks who live together, and the patron of a continent? The story begins in Rome, with a young student who looked at the life around him and, as Gregory puts it, drew back his foot.</p>

<h2 id="learned-ignorance-unlearned-wisdom">Learned Ignorance, Unlearned Wisdom</h2>

<p>Benedict came from the province of Nursia, born, Gregory says, “of honourable parentage,” and that is nearly all anyone knows of his family. Later writers gave his parents names and traced the family to a great Roman house, but Gregory, who had his story from men who had lived with Benedict, mentions neither. What Gregory does tell is that even as a boy he had, as it were, an old man’s mind in a young body. He was brought up in Rome and set to study letters, and what he saw there made him turn back.</p>

<p>Around him, other young men were learning the same books and sliding into dissolute lives, and the learning seemed to be carrying them down rather than lifting them. Benedict drew back his foot before he followed them. He left his books, he left his father’s house and its wealth, and he went out of the city. Gregory sums up the young man in one of the most quoted lines ever written about him: he went away “instructed with learned ignorance, and furnished with unlearned wisdom.” He had learned enough to know what he did not want.</p>

<p>He did not go entirely alone. His old nurse went with him, the only one of his household who followed. They stopped at a place the old English translation calls Enside, which is Effide, the modern Affile, and there Benedict lived for a while among a company of devout men, near the church of St. Peter.</p>

<p>At Effide a small household disaster struck. The nurse had borrowed a sieve from the neighbors, and it fell and broke in two. It was not hers, and she wept over it. Benedict gathered up the two pieces and, Gregory says, “with tears fell to his prayers.” When he rose, he found the sieve “so whole that the place could not be seen where before it was broken.”</p>

<p>The townspeople heard of it, and they hung the sieve over the door of their church, where it was still hanging, Gregory writes, “even to these very troubles of the Lombards,” that is, into his own day. It is the first wonder anyone told of Benedict, and it is a kind one: a young man who had walked away from a fortune prayed with tears over his nurse’s borrowed sieve.</p>

<p>It also spoiled his plan. He had left Rome to escape the world’s applause, and now a whole town was talking about him. So he stole away from Effide, from its admirers and even from his nurse, and went on alone toward a wild place called Subiaco.</p>

<h2 id="bread-on-a-rope">Bread on a Rope</h2>

<p>Subiaco lay almost forty miles from Rome, where the water of a spring had gathered into a lake. On the road there Benedict met a monk named Romanus, who belonged to a monastery nearby. Romanus kept his secret, gave him the habit of a monk, and became his servant in the strangest way. Benedict settled in a narrow cave, and for three years no one on earth knew he was there except Romanus.</p>

<p>Romanus stole what hours he could from his own monastery’s day, and saved what bread he could, and brought it to the hermit. He let it down to him on a long rope, with a little bell tied to the rope to call him. Gregory records that “the old enemy” once threw a stone and broke the bell. It is the first picture of community in Benedict’s life: a monk with a rope above, a young man in a cave below, and one brother quietly keeping another alive.</p>

<p>Three years in a cave is long enough to lose count of the days, and Benedict did. Far away, a priest had made ready a good dinner for himself when the Lord spoke to him in a vision: “Thou hast provided good cheer for thyself, and my servant in such a place is afflicted with hunger.” The priest set out, and on Easter Day he found the hermit in his cave. “Rise up, brother, and let us dine,” he said, for it was Easter. Benedict had not known. His answer is one of the gentlest lines in the whole of Gregory’s book:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“I know that it is Easter with me and a great feast, having found so much favour at God’s hands as this day to enjoy your company.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Benedict to the priest who found him in the cave, in Gregory, <em>Dialogues</em> II, chapter 1 (English of 1608)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>For a man who had fled to be alone, it is a revealing answer. The feast, for him, was a visitor.</p>

<p>Other visitors followed. Shepherds came upon him dressed in skins, and at first thought he was some beast. When they understood that he was a servant of God, many of them changed their lives, and word of the man in the cave began to spread through the countryside.</p>

<p>The cave did not protect him from himself. One day, Gregory says, a little black bird began to fly about his face. Soon afterward the memory of a woman he had once seen came back to him, and with it a temptation of the flesh. Near the cave grew briers and nettles. He stripped off his clothes, threw himself into them, and rolled there until his body was covered with wounds. “By the wounds of his body,” Gregory writes, “he cured the wounds of his soul.” Benedict told his disciples about it himself, years later, and he told them something else: after that day he never felt such a temptation again.</p>

<p>It is a hard story, and it is his own. The man who would one day write a rule that asks for nothing harsh or heavy had first learned, alone and in pain, how strong the enemy within can be. By now his solitude was ending. People knew where he was. One day a group of monks came to the cave with a request he did not want to grant.</p>

<h2 id="dwelling-with-himself">Dwelling With Himself</h2>

<p>They were the monks of a neighboring monastery, the ones who would later hand him the poisoned cup. Gregory does not name their house; later tradition placed it at Vicovaro. Their abbot had died, and they wanted the hermit in his place. Benedict refused for as long as he could, warning them that their manners and his could never agree. When he finally gave in, he did not soften. He asked them to live the life they had vowed, and they answered him with poison.</p>

<p>Gregory tells all this to his pupil Peter, who listens and asks questions all through the <em>Dialogues</em>, and here he stops to defend Benedict for leaving. A saint may leave a community that cannot be helped, he says; even St. Paul, he points out, was let down from Damascus in a basket. So Benedict went back to his cave and, in the phrase Gregory makes famous, “dwelt with himself,” <em>habitare secum</em>.</p>

<p>It did not last. Men came to the wilderness to live under him, and so many came that he built twelve monasteries, with twelve monks in each, “and a few he kept with himself.” The hermit had become the founder of a small world of monks among the hills around the lake.</p>

<p>Romans of rank began to bring him their sons to be raised in God’s service. A nobleman named Evitius gave him Maurus. A senator named Tertullus brought Placidus, “a boy of tender years.” Both boys will appear again and again in Benedict’s story.</p>

<p>Three of the twelve monasteries stood high on the mountain with no water of their own, and the monks were worn out with carrying it up. One night Benedict climbed the mountain himself with the boy Placidus, prayed there, and laid three stones to mark the place where he had prayed.</p>

<p>The next day he sent the monks up to dig where the three stones lay. They dug, and the rock began “sweating drops of water,” and then a spring broke out that flowed, Gregory says, “even to this day.” The abbot had answered their weariness with a night of prayer they never saw.</p>

<p>The boy who had climbed the mountain with him that night was the next one to need him, and very nearly too late.</p>

<h2 id="running-across-the-lake">Running Across the Lake</h2>

<p>Before that, there was the Goth. Among the men who came to Subiaco was a Goth, a man poor in spirit, as Gregory describes him, and Benedict received him into the community. In a monastery that held a senator’s son, the Goth was set to work with a billhook, clearing briers from the edge of the lake. As he worked, the iron head slipped off the handle and fell into the water, into a place “so deep, that there was no hope” of getting it back.</p>

<p>The Goth was miserable over it. Benedict came down to the shore, took the empty handle from him, and put it into the water. The iron rose from the bottom of the lake. Benedict handed the billhook back with a sentence that holds much of his character:</p>

<p>“Behold here is thy bill again, work on, and be sad no more.”</p>

<p>Gregory and Peter both thought of the prophet Elisha and his axe-head. But the tone of the story is Benedict’s own: no lecture on carelessness, no penance, only the tool returned and the work resumed and the sadness lifted.</p>

<p>Then came the day Placidus went down to the lake and fell in. The water caught him and carried him out from the shore “so far as one may shoot an arrow.” Benedict was in his cell, but he knew at once. He called Maurus and sent him running. Maurus ran down to the water and did not stop at the edge. He ran on across the surface of the lake, seized Placidus by the hair, and dragged him back to the shore.</p>

<p>When it was over, no one would take credit for it. Benedict said the wonder came from Maurus’s obedience. Maurus insisted it came from the abbot’s command; he had only done as he was told. It was Placidus, the one who had been in the water, who settled the matter. While he was being pulled out, he said, he had seen “the Abbot’s garment upon his head.”</p>

<p>Each man insisted the wonder belonged to the other, and it took the boy who had nearly drowned to decide it. At Subiaco, it seems, no one wanted the credit.</p>

<p>Not everyone who watched it grow was glad.</p>

<h2 id="the-crow-and-the-poisoned-loaf">The Crow and the Poisoned Loaf</h2>

<p>Not far from Subiaco stood a church whose priest was named Florentius. Gregory pauses to tell Peter that this man was the grandfather of “Florentius our sub-deacon,” a cleric of Gregory’s own church, so the story came close to home. The priest envied Benedict. The abbot’s monasteries were full, his name was spreading, and the priest nearby could not bear it.</p>

<p>Florentius sent Benedict a loaf of bread, and the bread was poisoned. Benedict knew. Every day a tame crow came to him and took bread from his hand, and when it came that day, he put the poisoned loaf in front of it and gave it an order:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“In the name of Jesus Christ our Lord, take up that loaf, and leave it in some such place where no man may find it.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Benedict to the crow, in Gregory, <em>Dialogues</em> II, chapter 8 (English of 1608)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The crow did not like the errand. It hopped about the loaf and cried out, and for a while it would not touch it. At last, “with much ado,” it took the loaf and flew away. Three hours later it was back, and took its usual bread from Benedict’s hand. A man had tried to murder him, and Benedict had answered with an errand for a bird.</p>

<p>Florentius tried again, and this time he aimed at the monks. He sent seven naked young women into the yard of the monastery to dance in front of the young brothers. Benedict, “fearing the danger” for his disciples, decided that he would be the one to go. He “gave place to envy,” Gregory says. With a few monks he left Subiaco and the monasteries he had built there.</p>

<p>Benedict was “scarce ten miles off” when word reached him from Maurus. Florentius was dead: the room where he was had collapsed and killed him. Maurus was glad of the news. Benedict was not. He wept for the man who had tried to poison him and to corrupt his young monks, and then he gave Maurus a penance for rejoicing at an enemy’s death.</p>

<p>Peter, hearing all this, was reminded of Moses, of Elisha, of St. Peter, of Elijah, and of David. But the most telling moment belongs to no prophet. It is the abbot, ten miles down the road, in tears for the man who had driven him out.</p>

<p>He did not go back. With his few monks he went on to a mountain above a town called Cassino, and there he began again.</p>

<h2 id="fire-on-apollos-mountain">Fire on Apollo’s Mountain</h2>

<p>“The town, which is called Cassino,” Gregory writes, “standeth upon the side of an high mountain.” On the height stood an ancient chapel where the country people still worshipped the god Apollo, with woods around it. Benedict beat the idol in pieces, overthrew the altar, and set fire to the woods. Inside the old temple he built an oratory dedicated to St. Martin, and on the very spot where Apollo’s altar had stood he built an oratory of St. John the Baptist. Then he went out and preached to the people of the countryside around.</p>

<p>That second oratory, raised where the altar of Apollo had been, will come back at the end of his story.</p>

<p>The old enemy, Gregory says, did not give up the mountain quietly. He began to appear to Benedict openly, in a form Benedict could see. The monks saw nothing, but they heard the voice. First it called him by name: “Blessed Bennet.” The old English calls him Bennet, and his Latin name, Benedictus, means blessed. Then the voice turned: “Cursed Bennet, and not blessed: what hast thou to do with me?”</p>

<p>The building of the monastery went on under this pressure, and Gregory tells a string of stories from those years. A great stone that the monks needed would not move until Benedict came and blessed it; the devil, Gregory says, had been sitting on it. When the monks dug up a brass idol, the kitchen suddenly seemed to be on fire, though the fire turned out to be only a phantom.</p>

<p>The worst came while a new wall was going up. The devil, Gregory says, threw it down, and it fell on a young monk, the son of a courtier. So badly had the stones broken his limbs and his very bones that his brothers brought him to the abbot in a sack. Benedict took him into his cell and prayed alone. In the very same hour the young monk was sound again, and Benedict sent him back to work on the wall.</p>

<p>The monks of Cassino were learning that their abbot saw more than they did. They were about to learn that he saw more than they wished.</p>

<h2 id="nothing-hidden-from-the-abbot">Nothing Hidden From the Abbot</h2>

<p>The rule of the house was that monks who went out did not eat outside the monastery. One day some brothers who had been out ate at the house of a devout woman. When they came home, Benedict asked where they had eaten. Nowhere, they told him. He answered, “Why do you tell an untruth? for did you not go into such a woman’s house? and eat such and such kind of meat, and drink so many cups?”</p>

<p>Gregory had a story of the same kind about the brother of Valentinian, one of his four witnesses. This brother was a layman who would not eat on his way to the abbey until he got there. Once he ate on the road, and when he arrived, Benedict told him so.</p>

<p>Some of the stories are funny. A lay brother named Exhilaratus, a man of Gregory’s own circle (“whom you know,” Gregory reminds Peter), once hid a flagon of wine in a bush. The abbot warned him: “Take heed, my son, that thou drinkest not of that flagon which thou hast hidden in the bush: but first be careful to bow it down, and thou shalt find what is within it.” Exhilaratus tipped the flagon over, and a snake leaped out.</p>

<p>A monk who preached to some nuns accepted a few handkerchiefs from them and tucked them into his habit. When he came home, the abbot asked, “How cometh it to pass, brother, that sin is entered into your bosom?” Another time a young monk, the son of a great man, stood holding a candle for the abbot, and as he stood there a proud thought rose in him. He said nothing. Benedict turned to him: “Sign your heart, brother, for what is it that you say? Sign your heart.”</p>

<p>Gregory does not make his hero all-knowing. He is careful to add that “the spirit of prophecy doth not always illuminate the minds of the prophets,” and Benedict was no exception. What these stories show is an abbot who watched over his monks closely enough to catch a lie, a hidden flagon, or a proud thought, and who often corrected them with a single question.</p>

<p>Some of the stories are about bread. In a year of famine in Campania, the monastery came down to its last five loaves. Benedict told the monks, “Indeed, to-day some want there is, but to-morrow you shall have plenty.” The next morning two hundred bushels of meal lay at the monastery door. Who brought them, and how, Gregory says, “is unknown to this very day.”</p>

<p>Benedict gave as freely as he received. In the same hard times Benedict ordered that the little oil the monastery had left be given to a subdeacon named Agapitus, who had asked for it. Gregory goes on to tell how a glass of oil was thrown from a window and did not break, and how a barrel in the abbey then filled until the oil ran over. Another time an honest man came to the abbey in trouble because he owed twelve shillings. Benedict had no money at all, so he prayed for two days. On the third day thirteen shillings were found lying on the chest where the monastery kept its corn, one more than the debt. Gregory had that story from Peregrinus, one of Benedict’s own disciples.</p>

<p>The sick and the troubled came too. Gregory heard from an honourable man named Anthony how a boy of his father’s household was cured of leprosy. A man who had been poisoned was healed at Benedict’s touch, by an abbot who had twice been the target of poison himself. A priest of Aquinum, possessed by an evil spirit, had been taken to the shrines of the martyrs, and none of them healed him, “to the end that the world might know what great grace was in the servant of God, Bennet.” Benedict freed him, and laid two conditions on him: never to eat meat, and never to seek higher orders. Years later the priest took orders anyway, and the old torment returned and stayed with him until he died.</p>

<p>The devil, in these stories, is never far away, and sometimes he is faintly ridiculous. Benedict once saw him dressed as a doctor, riding a mule and carrying a horn and a mortar, on his way to the monks “to give them a drench.” Before long one of the old monks was in his grip, and Benedict set him free.</p>

<p>Sometimes, Gregory says, the abbot was present where he was not. When Benedict sent monks to found a monastery at Terracina, the new abbot and his prior both saw him in their sleep, showing them how the buildings should be laid out. When they spoke to him about it afterward, he answered with a question of his own: “Did not I appear to either of you in your sleep?”</p>

<p>Stories like these traveled far beyond the mountain, and they reached the Goths.</p>

<h2 id="a-king-in-borrowed-shoes">A King in Borrowed Shoes</h2>

<p>Not every Goth who came to Cassino came to pray. Gregory tells of one named Zalla, a cruel man, who had a prisoner bound with cords. When Benedict looked at the bound man, the cords fell away, and Gregory marvels at a man who, “sitting still, did appease the fury of that cruel Goth.”</p>

<p>“In the time of the Goths,” as Gregory puts it, Totila was their king, and in the 540s he was campaigning in the south of Italy. He had heard that the abbot of Cassino had the spirit of prophecy, and he decided to test it. He sent word that he was coming. Then he dressed his guardsman Riggo in the king’s own shoes and royal robes, gave him three attendants, Vultericus, Rudericus, and Blindinus, and a royal escort, and sent him up the mountain as though he were Totila himself.</p>

<p>Benedict was sitting a little way off. When Riggo came within hearing, the abbot called out to him: “Put off, my good son, put off that apparel, for that which thou hast on, is none of thine.” Riggo fell flat on the ground, and so did everyone with him. They did not dare go nearer. They went straight back to the king and told him how quickly they had been found out.</p>

<p>Then Totila came himself. When he saw Benedict, he threw himself face down on the ground. Three times he was told to rise, and still he lay there, until Benedict went to him and lifted him up with his own hands. Then the abbot spoke to the king:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Much wickedness do you daily commit, and many great sins have you done: now at length give over your sinful life. Into the city of Rome shall you enter, and over the sea shall you pass: nine years shall you reign, and in the tenth shall you leave this mortal life.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Benedict to Totila, king of the Goths, in Gregory, <em>Dialogues</em> II, chapter 15 (English of 1608)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>From that day, Gregory writes, Totila “was nothing so cruel as before.” And it all came to pass. Totila entered Rome, crossed the sea to Sicily, and in the tenth year of his reign lost his kingdom and his life. Gregory, writing half a century later, knew how the story ended, and he told it as a prophecy fulfilled.</p>

<p>Another visitor, the bishop of Canosa, told Benedict that Totila would leave Rome empty of people. Benedict disagreed: “Rome shall not be utterly destroyed by strangers: but shall be so shaken with tempests, lightnings, whirlwinds, and earthquakes, that it will fall to decay of itself.” Gregory, writing in Rome, needed no proof of that: “we see before our eyes in this very city.” He is honest about his evidence even here. Honoratus, who reported the saying, had it from monks who heard it, not from Benedict himself.</p>

<p>One prophecy Benedict made in tears. A man named Theoprobus, whom he had brought to the faith and who knew him well, came into his cell one day and found him weeping bitterly. When Theoprobus asked why, Benedict told him:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“All this Abbey which I have built … are by the judgment of almighty God delivered to the gentiles, to be spoiled and overthrown: and scarce could I obtain of God to have their lives spared, that should then live in it.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Benedict to Theoprobus, in Gregory, <em>Dialogues</em> II, chapter 17 (English of 1608)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>He had built it all, and he could not save it. He had begged for only one thing, the lives of his monks, and he had barely been granted that. It happened as he said. Some decades after his death the Lombards came to Monte Cassino by night, while the monks slept, and plundered the abbey. They did not take a single monk. Gregory wrote that the prophecy was “proved most true.”</p>

<p>Those spared lives are the reason we know anything about Benedict at all. The monks of Cassino made their way to Rome, and their memories traveled with them. There, in 593, three years after he became pope, Gregory the Great sat down, at the request of monks of his household, to gather the wonders of the fathers of Italy, and he gave a whole book to Benedict. He wrote in hard years, with the Lombards in Italy, for people who needed comfort and courage. And he named the men he had his story from:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“All the notable things and acts of his life I could not learn; but those few, which I mind now to report, I had by the relation of four of his disciples: to wit, of Constantinus, a most rare and reverent man, who was next Abbot after him; of Valentinianus, who many years had the charge of the Lateran Abbey; of Simplicius, who was the third General of his order; and lastly of Honoratus, who is now Abbot of that monastery in which he first began his holy life.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Gregory the Great, <em>Dialogues</em> II, prologue (English of 1608)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Gregory filled his book with wonders. But when he wanted to show Peter where the real Benedict could be found, he pointed somewhere else. Benedict, he said, “wrote a rule for his monks, both excellent for discretion and also eloquent for the style.” Anyone who wanted to know how he lived could find it there, because he could not teach otherwise than he lived.</p>

<h2 id="a-little-rule-for-beginners">A Little Rule for Beginners</h2>

<p>The Rule survives, and it is the one place where Benedict speaks to us without Gregory standing between. Gregory gives no date for it, and no one knows at what point in his life Benedict wrote it. Its first word is <em>Obsculta</em>: Listen.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Listen, my son, to the master’s teaching, and turn the ear of your heart. Gladly welcome the advice of a loving father and carry it out, so that you return by the work of obedience to the One you left through the laziness of disobedience.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Benedict, <em>Rule</em>, Prologue (our translation from the Latin)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>It opens in the voice of a father speaking to a son. The whole of the monk’s life, as Benedict describes it, is a road home: obedience undoing, one day at a time, the old refusal that led away from God. “We must therefore set up a school of the Lord’s service,” he writes, <em>dominici scola servitii</em>. And then the man whose first monks had poisoned his wine sooner than live under his discipline wrote this: “In laying this down we hope to order nothing harsh or heavy.” If something seems a little strict, he goes on, do not run in fear from the way of salvation; “it cannot begin except with a narrow gate.” But the way does not stay narrow. “As life and faith advance, the heart widens, and one runs the way of God’s commands in the unspeakable sweetness of love.”</p>

<p>The fourth chapter lists what Benedict calls the tools of good works, seventy-odd short commands. It begins with the two great commandments, to love God with the whole heart, soul, and strength, and the neighbor as oneself. It runs through the works of mercy: “Refresh the poor. Clothe the naked. Visit the sick. Bury the dead. Help in trouble. Comfort the grieving.” In the middle stand four Latin words that the Rule will repeat, almost exactly, near its end: <em>Nihil amori Christi præponere</em>, put nothing before the love of Christ. The list ends with the tool for the days when all the others fail: “And never despair of God’s mercy.”</p>

<p>Where is all this work to be done? “The workshop where we do all this carefully,” Benedict writes, “is the monastery enclosure and stability in the community.” It is a striking demand from this man. Benedict himself had twice walked away from a community, once from monks who tried to kill him and once to spare his young monks from Florentius, and Gregory had to defend him for it. Yet his Rule asks the monk to stay, and on the fourth step of humility it describes a man who, in hard and unjust treatment, “quietly embraces patience in his heart, endures, and does not grow weary or leave.”</p>

<p>That ladder of humility fills the Rule’s seventh chapter. “We must raise the ladder that Jacob saw in his dream,” Benedict writes, and our life in this world is that ladder, with body and soul as its two sides. It has twelve steps. The first is to keep the fear of God always before one’s eyes and never forget it. The last leads somewhere fear cannot go. At the top, he promises, the monk “will come to that love of God which, being perfect, casts out fear.” From then on he will keep every rule “no longer from dread of hell, but from love of Christ, from good habit, and from delight in virtue.”</p>

<p>Then there are the guests. Benedict had been found in his cave by a priest with an Easter dinner and by shepherds, and visited on his mountain by a king in borrowed shoes. He gave a whole chapter of the Rule to visitors, and it begins:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Let all guests who arrive be received as Christ, because he will say: I was a stranger and you took me in.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Benedict, <em>Rule</em>, chapter 53 (our translation from the Latin)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>When guests arrive, the brothers bow their heads or lie stretched on the ground before them: “let Christ be adored in them, for Christ is who is received.” The abbot himself pours water over the guests’ hands, and “the abbot and the whole community wash the feet of all guests.” Poor people and pilgrims are to be welcomed with the greatest care of all, “because in them Christ is received more; for the rich, fear itself wins them honour.” And because guests arrive at all hours, the abbot and the guests have a kitchen of their own, so that the brothers are not disturbed by visitors, “who are never lacking in a monastery.” The dryness of that last phrase is pure Benedict.</p>

<p>He brings the same eye to the monastery’s storeroom. The cellarer, the monk who keeps its goods, must be wise and sober, “not a big eater,” and “a father to the whole community.” He must treat all the tools and goods of the monastery “as if they were the sacred vessels of the altar.” The abbot who once fished a Goth’s billhook out of a lake wrote that. He cares “with full attention for the sick, the children, the guests, and the poor,” knowing that he will give account for every one of them on the day of judgment. If he has nothing to give, “let him give a kind word,” and the reason follows: “let no one be troubled or saddened in the house of God.” It is the same voice that told the Goth to be sad no more.</p>

<p>Twice the Rule asks that all things be done with measure, <em>omnia mensurate</em>. It knows people. On wine it sighs: “We read that wine is not for monks at all,” Benedict admits; “but since monks of our times cannot be persuaded of that, let us agree at least not to drink to fullness.” A measure called a hemina each day is enough, he decides, thinking of the weakness of the sick. The care of the sick, he writes, comes before everything else, because Christ is served in them. Of the abbot he asks the hardest thing of all: “let him try to be loved more than feared,” and let him know that he must serve more than rule. When a newcomer knocks, the test is a single question, whether he seeks God in earnest, <em>si revera Deum quaerit</em>.</p>

<p>Work matters, too. “Idleness is the enemy of the soul,” the Rule warns, and monks live as monks should when they live by the work of their hands, as the fathers and apostles did; the day is divided among prayer, work, and sacred reading. The motto most often hung on his name, <em>Ora et labora</em>, pray and work, appears nowhere in the Rule. The scholar Terence Kardong traces it to a nineteenth-century book by a German abbot, Maurus Wolter.</p>

<p>Near the end, Benedict turns from the abbot and the cellarer to the brothers themselves. There is a bitter zeal, he writes, that separates from God and leads to hell, and a good zeal that separates from vice and leads to God and eternal life. This is how he describes the good zeal:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Let them try to outdo each other in honour. Let them bear each other’s weaknesses of body and of character with great patience. Let them race one another in obedience. Let no one follow what he thinks useful for himself, but what is useful for the other.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Benedict, <em>Rule</em>, chapter 72 (our translation from the Latin)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>A rule can tell a monk to be home before nightfall. It cannot tell him what to do when love asks him to stay. Late in his life, Benedict learned that from his sister.</p>

<h2 id="the-storm-his-sister-asked-for">The Storm His Sister Asked For</h2>

<p>First, though, Gregory tells of a day when Benedict was asked for something he did not want to give. He was coming back from work in the fields when he met a countryman at the monastery gate. The man had brought the body of his dead son and laid it there, and when he saw the abbot he cried out, “Give me my son, give me my son!” Benedict drew back:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Away, my good brethren, away: such miracles are not for us to work, but for the blessed Apostles: why will you lay such a burthen upon me, as my weakness cannot bear?”</p>
  <p><cite>— Benedict at the monastery gate, in Gregory, <em>Dialogues</em> II, chapter 32 (English of 1608)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Then he went to the child. He stretched himself over the small body and prayed: “Behold not, O Lord, my sins, but the faith of this man.” The child began to tremble. Benedict gave him back to his father, alive and well. Gregory is careful to say that this wonder came by prayer. Some things, he explains, Benedict did by power and some by prayer, and this one he asked for.</p>

<p>The next story Gregory tells is about a time when Benedict wanted something and did not get it. In Gregory’s words, “he would have had that thing, which yet he could not.”</p>

<p>Benedict had a sister, Scholastica, “dedicated from her infancy to our Lord.” Once a year she came to see him, and he came down to meet her at a house that belonged to the abbey, not far from the gate, bringing a few monks with him. They spent the day “in the praises of God and spiritual talk.” When night came on, they were still at table, and she begged him to stay until morning. He refused. He would not, Gregory says, “by any means tarry all night out of his Abbey.”</p>

<p>The sky was clear. Scholastica folded her hands on the table, laid her head on them, and prayed, weeping. At the moment she lifted her head, thunder broke over the house and the rain came down. Gregory has a phrase for it: “she lifted up her head and brought down the rain.” Benedict, the lawgiver, the man who saw through walls and read hidden thoughts, could only say, “God forgive you, what have you done?” His sister answered him:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“I desired you to stay, and you would not hear me, I have desired our good Lord, and he hath vouchsafed to grant my petition: wherefore if you can now depart, a God’s name return to your monastery, and leave me here alone.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Scholastica to Benedict, in Gregory, <em>Dialogues</em> II, chapter 33 (English of 1608)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>He could not depart. So the brother and sister spent the whole night awake, talking together of the things of God. Gregory’s judgment on the contest is one of the loveliest sentences in his book. Benedict had the rule on his side, and Scholastica had the stronger prayer, and the reason was simple: “she did more which loved more.”</p>

<p>Three days later Benedict was standing in his cell when he saw his sister’s soul leave her body and rise into heaven “in the likeness of a dove.” He sent monks to bring her body to the monastery, and he had it laid in the grave he had prepared for himself, in the oratory of St. John the Baptist, on the ground where the altar of Apollo had stood. Gregory explains why: “as their souls were always one in God whiles they lived, so their bodies continued together after their death.”</p>

<h2 id="the-whole-world-in-a-ray">The Whole World in a Ray</h2>

<p>Among the friends who came to Monte Cassino was Servandus, a deacon and the abbot of a monastery in Campania founded by Liberius. Once, during one of his visits, the two of them went to rest in a tower that had a single stair. Before the night office, Benedict rose, stood at the window, and prayed.</p>

<p>In the dead of night, a light came, far brighter than daylight. Then, as Benedict himself told it afterward, the whole world seemed to be gathered together under a single ray of the sun and set before his eyes. In that light he saw the soul of Germanus, bishop of Capua, carried up to heaven by angels in a globe of fire.</p>

<p>He called out to Servandus two or three times. Servandus came, and saw only “a little remnant of the light.” Theoprobus sent a messenger to Capua, and the messenger learned that Germanus had died at that very hour.</p>

<p>When Peter asked how such a thing could be, Gregory answered with one of the most famous sentences in his book: “all creatures be as it were nothing to that soul which beholdeth the Creator.” The young man who had left Rome had gone looking for God alone in a cave. Near the end of his life, at a tower window, he was shown the whole world at once, gathered in God’s light. He had not lost the world by leaving it.</p>

<p>His own end was near now, and he knew it.</p>

<h2 id="standing-with-hands-raised">Standing, With Hands Raised</h2>

<p>In the year he died, Benedict told some of his monks the day of his death. Those who were with him he told to keep it secret; to those who were far away he gave a sign by which they would know. Six days before the end, he had his grave opened, the grave where Scholastica already lay. Then a fever took hold of him.</p>

<p>On the sixth day he had his disciples carry him into the oratory, and there he received the Body and Blood of Christ. He was too weak to stand, and he stood:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Having his weak body holden up betwixt the hands of his disciples, he stood with his own lifted up to heaven, and as he was in that manner praying, he gave up the ghost.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Gregory the Great, <em>Dialogues</em> II, chapter 37 (English of 1608)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The man who went into a cave to be alone with God died on his feet, at prayer, held up by the hands of his monks.</p>

<p>Two of those monks, one in his cell at the monastery and the other far away, saw the same vision. A road ran from Benedict’s cell eastward into heaven, spread with rich hangings and bright with lamps, and a man there told them whose road it was: “This is the way by which the beloved servant of God, Bennet, is ascended up to heaven.”</p>

<p>They buried him in the oratory of St. John the Baptist, beside his sister, on the ground where Apollo’s altar had stood when he first came up the mountain. And Gregory adds that the cave at Subiaco, where he had first lived alone, “even to this very time, worketh miracles”: only lately, he writes, a woman out of her mind had slept in that cave and come out healed.</p>

<h2 id="cross-book-and-plough">Cross, Book, and Plough</h2>

<p>His abbey did not long outlive him. A few decades after his death the Lombards plundered Monte Cassino, as he had foreseen, and the monks escaped with their lives to Rome. The abbey was refounded in the eighth century, sacked by Saracens late in the ninth, and rose again. In France, the monks of Fleury on the Loire came to claim that his bones had been carried to them from the ruined abbey; the claim is first reported in the ninth century, and Monte Cassino disputes it.</p>

<p>On 15 February 1944, Allied bombers destroyed the abbey, and some 230 civilians died there. Three years later, on 21 March 1947, Pope Pius XII wrote that practically nothing had survived the destruction except the crypt where Benedict’s relics are kept. When the ruins were cleared, archaeologists found traces of the two oratories Gregory had described more than thirteen centuries earlier, St. Martin’s and St. John’s, where Apollo’s temple and altar had been.</p>

<p>The abbey was rebuilt. On 24 October 1964 Pope Paul VI consecrated its new church, and on the same day he declared Benedict principal patron of all Europe. Cardinals, bishops, heads of religious orders, and lay leaders from many parts of Europe had asked for it, and John XXIII had wished it before him. Pius XII had already called him the father of Europe. Paul VI’s letter, which begins with the words <em>Pacis nuntius</em>, messenger of peace, gathers Benedict’s work into three Latin words: <em>cruce, libro, aratro</em>, by the cross, the book, and the plough. In the East, too, he is remembered: the Orthodox commemorate him on 14 March, and their synaxarion tells his life from Gregory, the monk who “raised the dead, foretold the future.”</p>

<p>Many people first meet Benedict on a small medal. On one side he holds a cross and his Rule, with a cup beside him and a raven with a loaf. Gregory’s English says crow, and the cup and the loaf were two separate attacks by two different enemies, but the medal joins them. On the other side, letters run around a cross. They stand for Latin verses: May the sacred cross be my light; let not the dragon be my guide; step back, Satan, never urge vain things on me; what you offer is evil, drink the poison yourself.</p>

<p>The verses are medieval, and for a while even their meaning was lost. In 1647, at a witchcraft trial at Natternberg in Bavaria, the accused said they had no power over the nearby abbey of Metten, because the abbey was protected by the cross. The monks of Metten found crosses painted on their walls with these letters, and no one remembered what they meant. The answer turned up in a manuscript of 1415 at the abbey, which showed Benedict holding a staff topped with a cross and a scroll, with the words written out in full. Pope Benedict XIV approved the medal in 1741 and 1742.</p>

<p>For all these honors, the Rule itself never sounds like the work of a man who expected to be called the father of Europe. In its last chapter, Benedict calls it <em>hanc minimam inchoationis regulam</em>, this least little rule for beginners.</p>

<p>He had gone into a cave to be alone with God, and God kept sending him people: a monk with a rope, a priest with a dinner, shepherds, nobles’ sons, a Goth, a king, a sister. He died standing, held up by their hands. And in the little rule he left them, the chapter on how brothers should love one another ends with a line about where they are going. Let them put absolutely nothing before Christ, “who will lead us all together to eternal life.”</p>

<h2 id="a-note-on-the-sources">A Note on the Sources</h2>

<p>Almost everything known of Benedict’s life comes from one book, the second book of Gregory the Great’s <em>Dialogues</em>, written in 593 or 594, some fifty years after Benedict’s death, from the memories of four named disciples and a few others. Gregory gives no dates at all. The usual dates are later inferences: a birth about 480, the founding of Monte Cassino about 529, and Totila’s visit in 543, the one date the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> of 1907 calls certain. For the death, Gardner, the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, and Guérin give 21 March 543, the 1911 <em>Britannica</em> gives about 544, and popular modern references, such as Wikipedia, give 547; the sources do not agree. Gregory wrote to strengthen faith, and the historian F. Homes Dudden (1905) read many of his stories as legends that grew quickly in an age that expected wonders, calling the Cassino years “dimly discernible through a haze of legend.” A few scholars, from Huldreich Coccius in 1551 to Francis Clark in the 1980s, have doubted that Gregory wrote the <em>Dialogues</em> at all; Clark proposed a forger of the 680s. Paul Meyvaert and Adalbert de Vogüé, among others, answered him, and most scholars accept Gregory as the author; we know this debate only from abstracts and summaries.</p>

<p>Later tradition added things Gregory does not say. It named Benedict’s parents and made Scholastica his twin, a tradition the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em> says Bede accepted. It said he died on Holy Saturday, which, Guérin notes, does not fit the date of Easter in 543, and that Maurus carried the Rule to Gaul. Gregory does not name the monastery of the poisoned cup; Vicovaro is a later identification. Chapter numbers for Gregory follow the Latin division into 38 chapters; Luck’s 1880 printing of the 1608 English divides the book into 48. Quotations from the Rule are cited by chapter, because most verse numbers could not be confirmed.</p>

<h2 id="how-this-was-researched">How This Life Was Researched</h2>

<p>This life was written from 25 sources in English, Latin, and French. Its core is Gregory the Great’s <em>Dialogues</em>, Book II, the only early life, read in the English translation of 1608 in three printings. Beside it stands Benedict’s Rule, read in Latin and checked against Oswald Hunter Blair’s English of 1907. The researchers copied 25 passages of the Rule and 21 passages of Gregory for quotation, and the English of the Rule quoted here is our own. Two papal documents were read at the Vatican’s site: Pius XII’s encyclical of 1947 and Paul VI’s apostolic letter of 1964, the latter only in Latin. Dudden’s study of Gregory (1905), Gardner’s notes (1911), the <em>Catholic Encyclopedia</em>, the 1911 <em>Britannica</em>, Guérin’s French <em>Petits Bollandistes</em>, Ullathorne (1860), and two older accounts of the Benedict medal supplied the later record. Several sources were not read: the Latin text of Gregory’s <em>Dialogues</em>, Paul the Deacon, the Regula Magistri debate, and the work of Meyvaert and de Vogüé in full. Nor were the English translations of the Rule by McCann (1952) and Verheyen (1949), Bede, or the English text of <em>Pacis nuntius</em>.</p>

